WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.331
- All right, y'all, we're
back with Garth Brooks,

00:00:02.331 --> 00:00:04.629
and "Gratitude with Garth" in celebration

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:06.012
of Thanksgiving week.

00:00:06.012 --> 00:00:09.933
(upbeat music)
(audience applauding)

00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:12.600
- So community, it's a word
that kind of encompasses a lot.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.570
It could be like the community
you surround yourself with.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:16.680
It could be where you live.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.150
- Sure.
- Like what are you grateful

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:18.983
for about?
- Well, you know,

00:00:18.983 --> 00:00:20.250
your community on tour is the band

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.083
and crew, right?
- Yeah.

00:00:21.083 --> 00:00:21.916
- That's your second family.

00:00:21.916 --> 00:00:23.520
- Yeah, so now the crazy thing,

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.350
we're opening a Friends in Low Places Bar

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.450
down on Lower Broadway, right?
- I know, very excited.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.160
- Now all of a sudden, you think about,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.480
'cause I've always played in honky-tonks.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.980
We have our whole lives,

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.620
but the truth is the
community in a honky-tonk,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:35.610
in a bar,
- Yeah.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:37.890
- forgive me for using the word church,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.750
but this is where you
come to not be alone.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.430
This is where you come
to share your problems,

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.942
raise a beer together, celebrate,

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:46.170
mourn, maybe sometimes.
- Yeah, or get rowdy. What?

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.480
- Amen.
- Yeah. (chuckles)

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.520
So wait, you have a big show this weekend?

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:51.660
- Yes.
- Yes, it's the day

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.180
after Thanksgiving.
- Yes, Black Friday.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.860
So for the first time,

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.500
I read the NFL's doing a
Black Friday football game.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:59.333
- Yeah.
- I don't know

00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:00.450
how long it took us to think of that.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.150
- I know, but I just got into
football, so I'm so excited.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.880
- There you go, and so Amazon
always does the concerts

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.650
after the Thursday night football games.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.220
- Bless you, and they're doing the game

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.450
after the Friday night one.
- Yeah.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:13.950
- And they said, "Hey man,
would you do that concert?"

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.690
I said, "I would love to do it."

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.490
We're opening the bar
at the exact same time.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:18.630
So we're gonna go into Friends

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.700
in Low Places Bar the first time ever

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.710
and we're gonna do a dive
bar concert in there.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:23.586
We'll play some new music,

00:01:23.586 --> 00:01:24.419
and then of course,
- Oh, my God.

00:01:24.419 --> 00:01:25.252
- the old stuff.

00:01:25.252 --> 00:01:29.087
- I'm so mad I'm not gonna be
here. (audience applauding)

00:01:29.087 --> 00:01:31.290
That's gonna be incredible.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.570
- How do you get in?
You're gonna love this.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.160
We just launched the The BIG 615,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.320
the global country music
station, it's awesome.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.690
So what I love about it is
the only way you get in,

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:40.860
you just listen and you register.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.350
So the people that you're
gonna play for that night,

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.540
they don't have to have a lot of money,

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.610
they don't have to have a certain look

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:48.870
about 'em or where they work.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:50.285
It's just luck of the draw.

00:01:50.285 --> 00:01:52.500
- I love that.
- So it's gonna be fantastic.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.120
It's fun-
- You get the real fans.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:54.953
- Yeah, amen.
- The ones that really

00:01:54.953 --> 00:01:56.670
wanna be there, yeah.
- Amen, the friends

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.190
in low places, that's it.
- Yeah, I like it.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.023
We're all about connection in this show.

00:02:00.023 --> 00:02:02.250
It's like it's kind of my thing in life.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.390
I just love connecting with people

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.460
and talking and communicating.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.790
But what connections are you
like so grateful for in life?

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:09.870
Obviously, Miss Yearwood.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.240
- Yeah, I can tell you this, I
learned this a long time ago.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.340
The greatest gifts that
you will leave this planet

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.350
with are relationships, not money.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.080
It's not anything like that.
It's just relationships.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.300
And you never know when
you're gonna call on them.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.747
And people say, you know,

00:02:22.747 --> 00:02:24.180
"I can carry the world on my shoulders."

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.260
I'm telling you right now,

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.270
you cannot carry the
world on your shoulders

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.460
until someone has carried you.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:30.780
You just can't, man.
- Aww.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.530
- It's that whole empathy of
understanding how people are,

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:35.633
and that's what it's all about, man.

00:02:35.633 --> 00:02:37.440
- Empathy in general.
- I'm in for that.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.230
Empathy is a, we got a rule on the farm

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.450
that you can't say a word you can't spell.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.732
So I can't say that word again,

00:02:43.732 --> 00:02:45.597
but we get it once.
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:02:45.597 --> 00:02:47.550
But that is the word, yes.
- That's incredible.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:48.870
I love that rule.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.690
So radio, it wasn't really
important for me as a kid

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.460
'cause one, I couldn't
afford to buy a lot of music

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:54.293
when I was younger.

00:02:54.293 --> 00:02:55.260
Was radio really important

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:56.093
for you growing up?
- Yeah.

00:02:56.093 --> 00:02:57.513
Very, very important for me.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.750
It's one of those things,
you know, we had gas,

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:01.800
well, again, before you were born,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.320
we had gas wars and they
would give away stuff

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.450
to try and get you to come
over their gas stations

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.283
and stuff.
- Yeah.

00:03:07.283 --> 00:03:10.230
- And we got this transistor
radio and somehow,

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.450
I confiscated, it became mine.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.070
So you'd lay there in bed at night.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:17.340
We got the Opry on WKY-AM at that point,

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.028
- Yeah.
- and then there was

00:03:19.028 --> 00:03:21.270
a radio station called the
KATT that was coming out

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.100
that did all the rock classics.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.330
- Oh, yeah.
- And so you'd lay there

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:25.620
in bed at night while everybody's asleep

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:26.880
with that little thing in your ear.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.050
And that's what you'd go to bed with,

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:29.430
is just listening to music.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:30.690
- Yeah. Oh, me too.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.250
Well, speaking of like, finding music,

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:33.840
I love that we talked about it a little.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.030
We touched on it, you
have like three channels.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:37.470
- Yes.
- So the Tailgate one is

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:38.550
like more of a tailgate party.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:39.620
That's your vibe?
- It just-

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.090
- And 615 is-
- Country music,

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:43.747
the entire family,
- Yeah, I love that.

00:03:43.747 --> 00:03:45.600
- which I love, yes.
- 615 with Nashville.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:46.800
That's still my number.
- It makes sense.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.780
- Yeah, and then the other
one's The Garth Channel.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:49.920
What's the difference between that one?

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.630
- Garth Channel is just as screwed up

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.850
as you could possibly be.
- (laughs) That's my favorite.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.230
Yeah.
- It doesn't matter

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.053
because here's my theory.
- You play like James Taylor.

00:03:57.053 --> 00:03:58.260
You play everything, yeah.
- 100%.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:59.760
Great artists can follow great artists.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.830
Great songs can follow great songs.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.937
Merle Haggard and Beyonce
can play back to back.

00:04:04.937 --> 00:04:08.359
- I agree, I agree.
(audience applauds)

00:04:08.359 --> 00:04:10.265
- Great, so we do-
- You're gonna love mine.

00:04:10.265 --> 00:04:12.000
Yeah, that's the same thing.
(Garth laughs)

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.920
I love it. First of all,
y'all just singing over there.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.050
We didn't get to play the whole
thing, but like you, I mean,

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.240
speaking of harmonies
and just beautiful music

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.760
and everything, how do
you do that work harmony,

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.050
balance all that in your life?

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:22.977
How do you do that?

00:04:22.977 --> 00:04:24.870
- Oh, I think what you do is you just run.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:26.493
I mean, somebody's got a plan.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:29.580
Don't try and have your own plan.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.710
Somebody's got a plan, and
just look for your open doors.

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.750
Karma, our friendship together.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.430
I can't tell you how hard it was

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.987
and how long it took me to text you to go,

00:04:37.987 --> 00:04:40.800
"Hey, I got this new song
and I would love for you to,"

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.170
it's so hard for me to ask, okay?

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:46.830
- But I want-
- Do you wanna how long it was

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:48.004
for me to respond?

00:04:48.004 --> 00:04:49.740
(audience laughing)

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.680
We can check it on my
phone. I can look at it.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.150
And it was literally, I was like, "Oh,"

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:55.231
and I was like, "Yes!"

00:04:55.231 --> 00:04:57.210
I was like...
(audience laughing)

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:58.950
- All capital letters.
- All capitals

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.390
with exclamation points.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:01.950
- I gotta tell you, man.
- Honestly, what the hell?

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:03.930
- It doesn't matter how long
you've been in this business,

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:06.930
when someone you love
treats you back like that,

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:07.763
thank you.
- Oh, my God.

00:05:07.763 --> 00:05:10.906
- That was freaking awesome.
- I will be there anytime

00:05:10.906 --> 00:05:12.270
for you or Miss Yearwood.
- That was good.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.100
- Y'all are my favorites, man.
- It's good.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.800
- And the song, I said this earlier.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.650
This song, okay, it's called
"The Ship and the Bottle".

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:20.700
- Yes.
- It's so beautiful.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.920
I said yes before I even heard
the song, I'm pretty sure.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.140
And this song, like when
I went in the studio,

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.280
and first of all,

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.180
you producing me always makes me

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:30.374
one (beep) Christmas turkey.

00:05:30.374 --> 00:05:35.374
(audience laughing)
It's like I get so nervous.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.580
It's happened twice to me,

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.860
but I was so nervous and I
was just trying to be cool.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.680
And anyway, but how you had me sing,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.710
nobody lets me really just sing, you know,

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.330
and not always reach for the raptures,

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:49.500
not always like do all the,

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:52.260
and it made me feel as
cool as Linda Ronstadt,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.510
which is one of my favorite
singers and artist ever.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:56.790
And like that's what the
vibe is kind of very,

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:58.500
it feels like it could've been released

00:05:58.500 --> 00:05:59.769
in like the 70s.

00:05:59.769 --> 00:06:01.440
- Yeah.
- Like, yeah, it's so cool.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.270
- It feels good. It's just
a kind of a beachy thing.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:06.480
And what I love the saying
about it is just this guy feels

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.697
like he's holding a woman back, you know?

00:06:08.697 --> 00:06:09.960
- Mm.
- And then I start thinking

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.497
about my own relationship
with Miss Yearwood and I go,

00:06:11.497 --> 00:06:14.280
"No wonder this sounds so
natural to sing, right?"

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.380
'Cause it's just, it's so good.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:17.790
She's the ship that should be out

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:19.500
on the water and he's the bottle.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.650
He just likes to drink. (laughs)

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.570
- But also the imagery of that,

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:25.403
like keeping in the-
- It's fabulous.

00:06:25.403 --> 00:06:27.000
- Oh, it's so-
- So smart.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.470
- We have to do it, you were texting me,

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:29.820
you were like, "Should we sing it live?"

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.440
And I was like, "We
should do a little bit."

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:32.310
We should do a little bit of it.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:33.401
It's so beautiful.
- I will play this.

00:06:33.401 --> 00:06:37.530
- Okay, let's do it.
(audience applauding)

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.750
- Every album comes out, we
play for the songwriters.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:40.950
- Yeah.
- And there was a songwriter,

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:42.870
a young lady that was
in the chair listening.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:44.910
She's in the captain's chair hearing it.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:46.920
And I can tell she's
starting to get emotional

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.597
when the song's playing, and I'm going,

00:06:49.597 --> 00:06:51.327
"You still got it, right?"

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.660
Because she turns around and grabs my arm

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:56.617
with these beautiful eyes and goes,

00:06:56.617 --> 00:06:58.927
"My God, that's Kelly Clarkson, isn't it?"

00:06:58.927 --> 00:07:01.478
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:07:01.478 --> 00:07:04.139
- Which is never gonna happen.
- It's so fabulous.

00:07:04.139 --> 00:07:06.390
No, ma'am, when your voice
comes out on this record,

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:10.038
everybody goes and everybody
knows who it is too.

00:07:10.038 --> 00:07:12.180
- Whatever, but I love this song

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:13.580
and thank you for asking me.

